<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="65c20c1f">&lt;p&gt;
    Conducting a demo will result in immediate feedback from users and can assist in making sure the right solution is
    built. The feedback will provide a sense of whether the solution is on track or whether corrective actions needs to
    happen. In addition to feedback, potentials risk and dependencies should also be discussed and documented.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="20034bc5"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="ce2dd1ef"></attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="9c6ca543"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="c3c5c921">To provide a place to capture the level of acceptability of the developed solution, and obtain input on any
modifications needed.</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="3e40e225">If user feedback is not collected, the project team risks developing in a &quot;vacuum&quot;, especially for requirements
that are complex, which could lead to developing a solution that will not be accepted by users.</attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="12bfdf31">If the requirements are simple to develop, or are well-documented, user feedback may not be necessary.</attribute>
